Attached are some pics of my adventures last night.

Also attached are some pics of what I think the problem was.

You see I place for these in series to get a nice 12 volts right?

Everything was fine until I put this on the metal plate after I cleaned out=
 the ammo box which had this pack last night.

I believe my grievous error was not realizing that the bottom of these batt=
eries are the negative terminal as well.

Doing this with one battery is not an issue, but the problem is when these =
batteries are in series and I shorted the negative of a battery down the li=
ne to the metal plate the "magic" started.  Considering the batteries are i=
n series the negative terminal was not zero down the line. So if it wasn't =
zero there was a voltage difference between the negative terminals.  I thin=
k this ended up causing a short through the negative terminals not the posi=
tive terminals.  If I had placed them flat instead of on the negative termi=
nals on the metal plate I suspect this would not have happened so this appe=
ars to be my grievous error what does everyone think?

Next time I run these in series, and some of these batteries might still be=
 okay actually I will find out shortly, I'd better make sure I tape the bot=
toms of the negatives so that I don't get some unintentional shorts again.

I must have had the presence of mind to knock these onto their sides to rem=
ove the short which at least caused the source of the fire.

Mike
K6MSM

